  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The chemical formula for washing soda is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>.</mo><mn>10</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math>, which means that each molecule of washing soda contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn></math> water molecules.</p><p>The chemical formula for soda ash is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>, which does not contain any water molecules.</p><p>Therefore, there are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn></math> water molecules in washing soda and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#160;</mo></math>water molecules in soda ash.</p>",
